 {"acceptance_criteria": "1) A stated policy: either an enforced/documented maximum argument size with an explicit, clear refusal above it, or a documented linear-cost budget with the measured curve. 2) A committed benchmark (small, bounded, runnable on a laptop) that reports p50/p99 for a small, an 8 KB, and a 20 KB argument, plus a ceiling assertion so a regression beyond the pinned numbers fails. 3) The policy is visible where an integrator reads it (README/bridge docs), not only in a test. 4) Regression coverage for the TJ-DF-024 class stays green under the new guard.", "attempts": 0, "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on the fresh clone (n=200 each): p50 21.8 ms for an 8 KB argument and 51.7 ms for a 20 KB argument, against 2.2 ms for ordinary commands — cost grows linearly with argument size, so a host that pipes whole files through the firewall pays seconds per call. TJ-DF-024 (complete) fixed the acute case (one 8 KB argument cost ~7.5 s of CPU inside intercept(); now 22 ms), but nothing states a policy or pins the behaviour: there is no documented maximum, no guard against the size that makes a host stall, and no benchmark in CI. The report's framing: fine for a shell command, a real ceiling for file-sized input.", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "id": "TJ-GAP-086", "priority": "P3", "status": "complete", "title": "[P3] Argument-size ceiling is unbounded and linear: 8 KB = 22 ms, 20 KB = 52 ms, no documented policy or guard", "type": "task", "worker_status": "complete", "commit_hash": "80deb8e", "completed_at": "2026-10-07T21:49:37Z", "updated_at": "2026-10-07T21:49:37Z", "foreman_note": "Closed tick terminal-jail-foreman-2026-10-07-20-23-48. Worker glm-5.3-flash@zai-glm-default (serial dispatch, shared workdir), commit 80deb8e pushed to origin/main (parity 0). Option (a): enforced max TERMINAL_JAIL_INTERRUPTOR_MAX_ARG_BYTES default 262144, clean BLOCK rule_id builtin-max-arg-bytes checked before parsing; warn degrades to allow-with-warning; invalid/zero knob falls back to default. Benchmark scripts/arg_size_benchmark.py (small/8KB/20KB p50-p99, live run: 0.4/53.1/131.1 ms p50). Ceiling test tests/test_arg_size_ceiling.py; policy pinned in README env table + docs/quickstart.md. 29 new policy/ceiling tests; full suite 1910 passed/10 skipped at HEAD; ruff check+format clean; guard tier1 PASS (guard-20261007T213936). gitreins tier2 judge 402 deepseek (judged nothing, verdict 57bcb4f6) -> complete --skip-tier2 fast-flip per GITR-JUDGE-001; compensating evidence = full suite + guard + live benchmark at pushed SHA. ch:trace row=TJ-GAP-086 spec=README.md#environment-variables-and-limits test=tests/test_arg_size_ceiling.py evidence=commit 80deb8e on origin/main witness=live:arg-size-benchmark-run-at-HEAD commit=80deb8e", "guard_result": "tier1 PASS (guard-20261007T213936); tier2 402 -> skip-tier2 fast-flip; full suite 1910/10 at pushed HEAD"}
 {"acceptance_criteria": "1) install.sh locates its own checkout (BASH_SOURCE-based) and works when invoked by absolute path from any cwd; the failure message, when a checkout genuinely is absent, says which path it looked for. 2) A post-install capability check runs the pidns + fs-isolation probes and prints, in the install summary, which containment tier the host will actually get — with the remediation text when DEGRADED. 3) The check is advisory (never blocks a successful install) unless the user explicitly demands a tier, in which case it fails closed. 4) Tests: absolute-path invocation from a foreign cwd installs correctly; a DEGRADED host's install output contains the tier line and the remediation hint.", "attempts": 0, "commit_hash": "12966ff", "completed_at": "2026-10-04T05:50:07Z", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "description": "Verified twice on 2026-09-27: `HOME=<scratch> /path/to/repo/install.sh` run from another directory exits non-zero with 'no local checkout detected ... Supported install: run ./install.sh from a repository checkout' even though the script is sitting inside the checkout — the installer resolves ../ from $PWD instead of BASH_SOURCE, so the natural invocation (absolute path, from anywhere) silently installs nothing. Second half, same theme: the installer never checks or reports what the host can actually enforce. The project's own scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py prints an excellent, actionable remediation for the DEGRADED case ('install newuidmap/newgidmap plus /etc/subuid|/etc/subgid ranges for the calling user', or the root sysctl), but nothing runs it at install time — so an operator finishes a successful install believing they have filesystem containment when on Ubuntu they do not.", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "foreman_note": "Closed tick terminal-jail-foreman-2026-10-04-04-11-26 (wave worker glm-5.3-flash@zai-glm-default + foreman completion). Merged wt/TJ-GAP-087 as 9ee4da8, pushed origin/main parity 0. Judge tier2 PASS verdict 2eb7a2f3 (.gitreins/history/2026-10-04/2eb7a2f3). | FOLLOWUP (foreman tick 2026-10-04-04-11-26, this foreman): the sibling merge carried only 12966ff; rework commit 15f90d4 (cherry-picked as 933ffe0, pushed) adds the pidns-capability-probe tier line AND the TERMINAL_JAIL_REQUIRE_TIER fail-closed demand (valid values pidns|fs|full; demand unmet -> non-zero exit naming the tier; unset stays advisory) — acceptance criteria 2/3 now fully satisfied; plugin/test_install.py 86 passed, full plugin suite 1493 passed/4 skipped on 933ffe0.", "guard_result": "tier1 DEGRADED PASS on commit (secrets+lint+diff-tests)", "id": "TJ-GAP-087", "priority": "P3", "status": "complete", "title": "[P3] install.sh resolves the checkout from $PWD and reports the environment dishonestly: invoked by absolute path it installs nothing, and it never tells the operator which containment tier they got", "type": "task", "worker_status": "complete", "worker_summary": "install.sh resolves the checkout from the script location (absolute-path invocation from any cwd now installs; explicit TERMINAL_JAIL_USE_RELEASE=1 suppresses checkout adoption so release mode keeps its contract); post-install containment-tier report via scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py (advisory, degrades to one honest line). Worker timed out at 25min with tests-only changes; foreman completed the install.sh fix in the same worktree. Live probes: absolute-path install to scratch HOME succeeds + tier report printed (DEGRADED AppArmor userns verdict); lone-script copy outside checkout still refuses honestly. plugin/test_install.py 77 passed, test_uninstall.py 13 passed."}
 {"acceptance_criteria": "1) The preflight distinguishes 'uname is not on PATH' from 'this is not Linux' and says so, naming the missing tool (or reads /proc/version as a fallback so the check cannot be defeated by a thin PATH). 2) Same treatment for the other preflight binaries it depends on, at minimum unshare (already named) — a missing dependency must never be reported as a platform limitation. 3) A test runs the CLI with a PATH containing only the minimum, asserting the diagnostic names the missing tool and that the exit code still fails closed.", "attempts": 0, "commit_hash": "cd31f7b", "completed_at": "2026-09-29T10:20:00-05:00", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "description": "Verified 2026-09-27 by running the installed CLI with a deliberately crippled PATH: the wrapper's preflight does `if [ \"$(uname -s)\" != \"Linux\" ]` and, when `uname` is absent, the command substitution is empty, so the wrapper exits 2 printing 'terminal-jail: this CLI requires Linux PID namespaces' (plus a stray 'line 452: uname: command not found' from the shell). The operator is sent to investigate their kernel when the real problem is a stripped PATH (containers, minimal images, sandboxed exec wrappers). Found while reviewing; the bwrap-absent probe had to be rebuilt with a fuller PATH to get an uncontaminated result, which is exactly the confusion a user would hit.", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "guard_result": "Tier1 DEGRADED PASS (secrets+static_analysis clean; lint/tests skipped, nothing staged at post-commit guard)", "id": "TJ-DF-038", "priority": "P3", "status": "complete", "title": "[P3] Missing `uname` on PATH is diagnosed as 'this CLI requires Linux PID namespaces' — a Linux host told it is not Linux", "type": "task", "updated_at": "2026-09-29T10:20:00-05:00", "worker_status": "complete", "worker_summary": "Preflight: uname-absent falls back to /proc/sys/kernel/ostype then /proc/version before claiming non-Linux; if neither verifies, names uname as missing (install coreutils) and exits 2 fail-closed. 8 tests added (plugin/test_standalone_cli.py), full plugin suite 1454 passed / 7 skipped. Live probe PATH=/tmp/bare (no uname): help renders normally on this Linux host."}
