 {"acceptance_criteria": "1) One place (README section + docs) holds a capability matrix: rows = the enforcement layers (PID namespace, identity/env scrub, private /proc, filesystem isolation, seccomp, egress), columns = the backends and/or host classes, cells = enforced / degraded / unavailable with the reason. 2) The commands that produce the read for a given host (scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py, scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py) are named in the same place, so a reader can generate their own row. 3) The Ubuntu AppArmor case is stated in the matrix AND in the adoption guidance, with the remediation, so 'filesystem isolation: unavailable on Ubuntu 24.04+ by default' is not discoverable only by reading a warning at runtime. 4) Claims are pinned by tests/docs checks as the repo already does elsewhere (no drifting numbers).", "attempts": 0, "commit_hash": "5a7faaa", "completed_at": "2026-10-04T05:50:07Z", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "description": "The README is unusually honest about individual limitations (private /proc is bubblewrap-only; the filesystem tier needs a uid mapping; the unshare path exposes host /proc) — the strongest trust signal in the project, and the review says so. But those limits are spread across prose, COMPATIBILITY.md and docs/backend-parity.md, so a reader cannot answer the one question that decides adoption: 'on MY host, with MY kernel and distro, which of the three layers actually enforce?' The measured 2026-09-27 answer for Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31 — PID namespace YES, identity scrub YES, seccomp YES (with --seccomp), private /proc only under bwrap, filesystem isolation NO (DEGRADED) — is exactly the kind of table the project should print, next to the two probes that generate it.", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "foreman_note": "Closed tick terminal-j